# Artifacts: cached constants

Dmytro: "an artifact is a constant whose value is cached, e.g. deusCli := Deus.toBinary Cli.main;
evaluating it once caches the result in the artifacts layer by the hash of the expression" (task
88004c30). A constant marked `@[artifact]` is evaluated once and its value is kept in the artifacts
layer under the constant's hash. The hash covers the constant's body and, through its references,
everything it is computed from, so a change to any of those gives a new hash and the next use evaluates
it again; nothing is ever invalidated by hand. Whatever the evaluation reads from outside the
codebase (files, tools, the network) is not in the hash. Written in the order of the process (D92).

## Extensions

| Term | URI |
| --- | --- |
| `api` | `org.dxos.deus.api@1.0` |
| `req` | `org.dxos.deus.req@1.0` |

## APIs

```mdl
api A-1: cached constants
  signatures:
  - Cli.ensureArtifacts : Text -> List Text -> {IO, Throw Text} List Text
  - Cli.cachedValue : Text -> Text -> {IO} Option Sexp
  - Cli.artifact : Args -> {IO, Throw Text} Unit
```

## Requirements

```mdl
req R-1: deus artifact <name> evaluates an @[artifact] constant and keeps its value under the constant's hash in the artifacts layer
  by: Artifact.Spec.artifactKeepsItsValue
```

```mdl
req R-2: the first deus run of a program fills the cache for each @[artifact] constant it uses, evaluating it once
  by: Artifact.Spec.runFillsTheCache
```

```mdl
req R-3: a change to anything the constant is computed from gives it a new hash, so the next use evaluates it again
  by: Artifact.Spec.changeEvaluatesAgain
```

```mdl
req R-4: an image of a program that uses a cached constant holds its value, not the definitions it was computed from
  by: Artifact.Spec.imageHoldsTheValue
```

```mdl
req R-5: only a first-order constant can be an artifact: a function, or a definition with parameters, is refused by name
  by: Artifact.Spec.functionsAreRefused
```

## Laws

Each law runs the CLI on a codebase in the deterministic simulation; `deus ls Artifact.Spec.` lists them. The laws run in one
machine, which keeps a constant's value once computed, so each law uses its own program text and so its own hashes.
